  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 300 AM CDT Wed Sep 16 2026\n\nToday through Tuesday: An unusually strong subtropical high (H500\n600 dam, SD 3+) for late September will be the primary influence \non our region's weather through the entire forecast period. The \nonly exception to the ridge dominance will be brief interaction \nwith an upper trough that is expected to meander westward across \nthe Florida peninsula this weekend. Given the strength of the \nridge, daytime temperatures will continue to be well above normal,\nand a few record highs could be broken, especially over the \nnorthwest half of the forecast area. Fortunately, in terms of \nheat stress, increased low level dry easterly flow and strong \ndaytime mixing will result in lower relative humidity and reduced\nheat indices. \n\nThere is some disagreement among the global ensembles with how \nfar west the upper low center and it's sphere of influence will \nreach, but the consensus as of now is for a small increase in \ndiurnally driven afternoon showers and storms this weekend. A \ntrend to monitor will be late Saturday thunderstorm chances over \nsoutheast portions of the forecast area in association with \nincreased deep layer moisture on periphery of the upper low. As of\nnow, we are indicating very limited rain chances in the official \nforecast. Low to medium diurnally shower and storm chances can be\nexpected net week. /EC/",
